 No exceptions without the user's explicit permission.
+
+## Pitfalls
+
+1. **Writing implementation before the test**: If the test passes immediately without any implementation code, you skipped the RED phase. Confirm a FAILED result from pytest before writing a single line of implementation.
+2. **Trivially passing assertions**: Writing assert result is not None when you mean to verify a specific value gives false confidence. Assert the exact expected value.
+3. **Testing implementation details instead of behavior**: Tests that access private methods break on every refactor. Test only public API and observable outputs.
+4. **Skipping the REFACTOR phase**: Leaving code green but ugly accumulates technical debt. After every GREEN pass, clean up and re-run tests.
+5. **Shared mutable state between tests**: A global variable modified in one test bleeds into the next, causing order-dependent failures. Use pytest fixtures with yield for teardown.
+6. **Mocking code you own**: Patching your own internal functions couples tests to implementation details. Only mock external I/O boundaries.
+7. **Writing the entire test suite before any implementation**: TDD is one cycle at a time. Writing 20 failing tests at once eliminates the design feedback loop.
